{"question_id":"mnswhx4b","paperTitle":"JEE Main 2026 (Online) 5th April Morning Shift","year":2026,"subject":"physics","chapter":"heat-and-thermodynamics","chapterGroup":"mechanics","type":"mcq","marks":4,"negMarks":1,"content":"<p>Consider the following statements:</p>\n<p>A. Zeroth law of thermodynamics gives concept of temperature</p>\n<p>B. First law of thermodynamics gives concept of internal energy</p>\n<p>C. In isothermal expansion of ideal gas, <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.486ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"10.947ex\" height=\"2.106ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -716 4838.6 931\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(833,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D444\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D444\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1901.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"2260\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2260\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(2957.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(3790.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D44A\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D44A\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container></p>\n<p>D. Product of intensive and extensive variables is extensive</p>\n<p>E. The ratio of any extensive variable to mass will be an extensive variable</p>\n<p>Choose the correct combination of statements from the options given below:</p>\n","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"<p>C, D and E Only</p>\n"},{"identifier":"B","content":"<p>A, B and C Only</p>\n"},{"identifier":"C","content":"<p>A, B and D Only</p>\n"},{"identifier":"D","content":"<p>B, C and D Only</p>"}],"correct_options":["C"],"explanation":"<p>The Zeroth Law states that if two systems are each in thermal equilibrium with a third system, they are in thermal equilibrium with each other, i.e., the Zeroth law gives idea about the property of temperature to be measured. Hence, statement A is true.</p>\n<p>The First Law is the mathematical relation between heat ( <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.437ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.645ex\" height=\"2.057ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -716 1611 909\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(833,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"51\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-51\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> ), change in internal energy ( <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.581ex\" height=\"1.67ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -716 1583 738\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(833,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"55\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-55\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> ) and work done by the system ( <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"4.21ex\" height=\"1.67ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -716 1861 738\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(833,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"57\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-57\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> )</p>\n<p><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.437ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"17.219ex\" height=\"2.057ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -716 7611 909\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(833,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"51\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-51\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1888.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(2944.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(3777.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"55\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-55\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(4749.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(5750,0)\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(6583,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"57\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-57\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container></p>\n<p>This relation introduces internal energy ( <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.735ex\" height=\"1.595ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 767 705\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D448\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D448\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> ) as a state function which is based on energy conservation. Hence, statement B is true.</p>\n<p>In an isothermal expansion of an ideal gas, temperature remains constant, so <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"7.768ex\" height=\"1.67ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -716 3433.6 738\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(833,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D448\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D448\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1877.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(2933.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-30\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>.</p>\n<p>From the First Law: <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.437ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"49.9ex\" height=\"2.057ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -716 22055.7 909\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(833,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"51\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-51\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1888.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(2944.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(3777.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"55\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-55\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(4749.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(5750,0)\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(6583,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"57\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-57\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(7888.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"21D2\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-21D2\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(9166.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(9999.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"51\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-51\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(11055.3,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(12111.1,0)\"><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-30\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(12833.3,0)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(13833.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(14666.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"57\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-57\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(15972.3,0)\"><use data-c=\"21D2\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-21D2\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(17250.1,0)\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(18083.1,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"51\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-51\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(19138.9,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(20194.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(21027.7,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"57\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-57\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>.</p>\n<p>But as per statement <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.719ex\" height=\"1.645ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -705 760 727\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D436\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D436\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>, in isothermal process <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.486ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"10.947ex\" height=\"2.106ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -716 4838.6 931\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(833,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D444\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D444\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1901.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"2260\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2260\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(2957.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(3790.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D44A\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D44A\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>, so statement <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.719ex\" height=\"1.645ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -705 760 727\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D436\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D436\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> is False.</p>\n<p>Intensive variables are the properties which do not depend on size, for example temperature and pressure.</p>\n<p>Extensive variables are the properties depend on size, for example volume.</p>\n<p>The product of intensive and extensive property <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"6.003ex\" height=\"1.595ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 2653.4 705\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"50\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-50\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(903.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"D7\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-D7\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(1903.4,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"56\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-56\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> represents work or energy, which depends with the size of the system. Therefore, the product is extensive.</p>\n<p>When an extensive variable (like volume or heat capacity) is divided by mass, it becomes a specific quantity (like specific volume or specific heat). Specific quantities are intensive because they do not depend on the total mass of the system. Statement E says it remains extensive, so it is False.</p>\n<p>Therefore, only A, B, and D are correct. Hence, the correct option is (C).</p>","qKey":"mnswhx4b","paperKey":"jee-main-2026-online-5th-april-morning-shift","exam":"jee-main","questions":[{"question_id":"mnswhx4b","paperTitle":"JEE Main 2026 (Online) 5th April Morning Shift","year":2026,"subject":"physics","chapter":"heat-and-thermodynamics","chapterGroup":"mechanics","type":"mcq","marks":4,"negMarks":1,"content":"<p>Consider the following statements:</p>\n<p>A. Zeroth law of thermodynamics gives concept of temperature</p>\n<p>B. First law of thermodynamics gives concept of internal energy</p>\n<p>C. In isothermal expansion of ideal gas, <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.486ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"10.947ex\" height=\"2.106ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -716 4838.6 931\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(833,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D444\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D444\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1901.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"2260\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2260\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(2957.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(3790.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D44A\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D44A\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container></p>\n<p>D. Product of intensive and extensive variables is extensive</p>\n<p>E. The ratio of any extensive variable to mass will be an extensive variable</p>\n<p>Choose the correct combination of statements from the options given below:</p>\n","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"<p>C, D and E Only</p>\n"},{"identifier":"B","content":"<p>A, B and C Only</p>\n"},{"identifier":"C","content":"<p>A, B and D Only</p>\n"},{"identifier":"D","content":"<p>B, C and D Only</p>"}],"correct_options":["C"],"explanation":"<p>The Zeroth Law states that if two systems are each in thermal equilibrium with a third system, they are in thermal equilibrium with each other, i.e., the Zeroth law gives idea about the property of temperature to be measured. Hence, statement A is true.</p>\n<p>The First Law is the mathematical relation between heat ( <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.437ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.645ex\" height=\"2.057ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -716 1611 909\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(833,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"51\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-51\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> ), change in internal energy ( <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.581ex\" height=\"1.67ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -716 1583 738\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(833,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"55\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-55\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> ) and work done by the system ( <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"4.21ex\" height=\"1.67ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -716 1861 738\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(833,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"57\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-57\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> )</p>\n<p><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.437ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"17.219ex\" height=\"2.057ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -716 7611 909\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(833,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"51\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-51\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1888.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(2944.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(3777.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"55\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-55\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(4749.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(5750,0)\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(6583,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"57\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-57\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container></p>\n<p>This relation introduces internal energy ( <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.735ex\" height=\"1.595ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 767 705\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D448\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D448\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> ) as a state function which is based on energy conservation. Hence, statement B is true.</p>\n<p>In an isothermal expansion of an ideal gas, temperature remains constant, so <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"7.768ex\" height=\"1.67ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -716 3433.6 738\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(833,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D448\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D448\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1877.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(2933.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-30\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>.</p>\n<p>From the First Law: <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.437ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"49.9ex\" height=\"2.057ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -716 22055.7 909\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(833,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"51\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-51\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1888.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(2944.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(3777.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"55\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-55\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(4749.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(5750,0)\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(6583,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"57\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-57\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(7888.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"21D2\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-21D2\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(9166.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(9999.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"51\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-51\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(11055.3,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(12111.1,0)\"><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-30\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(12833.3,0)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(13833.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(14666.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"57\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-57\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(15972.3,0)\"><use data-c=\"21D2\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-21D2\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(17250.1,0)\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(18083.1,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"51\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-51\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(19138.9,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(20194.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(21027.7,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"57\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-57\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>.</p>\n<p>But as per statement <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.719ex\" height=\"1.645ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -705 760 727\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D436\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D436\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>, in isothermal process <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.486ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"10.947ex\" height=\"2.106ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -716 4838.6 931\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(833,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D444\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D444\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1901.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"2260\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2260\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(2957.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(3790.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D44A\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D44A\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>, so statement <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.719ex\" height=\"1.645ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -705 760 727\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D436\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D436\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> is False.</p>\n<p>Intensive variables are the properties which do not depend on size, for example temperature and pressure.</p>\n<p>Extensive variables are the properties depend on size, for example volume.</p>\n<p>The product of intensive and extensive property <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"6.003ex\" height=\"1.595ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 2653.4 705\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"50\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-50\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(903.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"D7\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-D7\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(1903.4,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"56\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-56\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> represents work or energy, which depends with the size of the system. Therefore, the product is extensive.</p>\n<p>When an extensive variable (like volume or heat capacity) is divided by mass, it becomes a specific quantity (like specific volume or specific heat). Specific quantities are intensive because they do not depend on the total mass of the system. Statement E says it remains extensive, so it is False.</p>\n<p>Therefore, only A, B, and D are correct. Hence, the correct option is (C).</p>","qKey":"mnswhx4b","paperKey":"jee-main-2026-online-5th-april-morning-shift","exam":"jee-main"}]}